    "//#expo:doctor": {
      "description": "Root-level task: run EXPO'S OWN dependency oracle against every Expo app, and classify what it finds by severity. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only: it spans two workspaces and shells out to a CLI that belongs to neither. ROOT CAUSE THIS CLOSES, and it is a WRONG-ORACLE bug rather than a missing check. //#knip reported expo-system-ui as an UNLISTED dependency of both Expo apps, inferred from userInterfaceStyle:automatic in app.json -- a reasonable inference, and wrong: expo-doctor run against the same tree reports 19/20 checks passing with NO missing peer dependency at all. knip answers a JS IMPORT-GRAPH question, and Expo native-module correctness is not an import-graph question. Since SDK 54 autolinking links modules from an app's DIRECT AND NESTED DEPENDENCIES rather than scanning node_modules, so whether a native module is present, declared and version-matched is decided by rules only Expo's tooling models. Acting on knip's answer would have added a dependency nothing needs. WHAT THE RIGHT ORACLE FOUND ON ITS FIRST RUN: 15 packages drifted from the installed SDK pins, including expo itself (55.0.26 vs ~55.0.29) and react-native (0.83.6 vs 0.83.10). None of that is visible to knip, eslint, tsc or any other gate this repo owns; it accumulated silently because NOTHING ASKED. A June session proved the value by hand -- it found 'Missing peer dependency: expo-constants, Required by: expo-router', which was fixed and is declared today -- and then nothing made the check repeat, the decorative-control shape this repo has now closed five times. GRADED, NOT BOOLEAN, and that is the load-bearing design decision. A version drift and a MISSING NATIVE PEER are not the same severity: Expo documents the latter as 'your app may crash outside of Expo Go', while the former is hygiene. Collapsing them forces a choice between a gate born red on 15 patch drifts -- which teaches everyone to bypass it, the adoption failure //#typecheck:scripts and //#knip both document -- and no gate at all. So a missing peer BLOCKS and drift REPORTS, because the drift fix rewrites versions mobile-native-bundle-config.test.ts and eas-config.test.ts deliberately pin and belongs in its own reviewable bump. RUNS expo-doctor@latest VIA npx, deliberately not a declared devDependency: its checks track the SDK, so a lockfile-pinned copy would answer with last quarter's rules about this quarter's SDK -- the stale-instrument failure //#prod:db-url exists to prevent. --yes suppresses the install prompt so it can never block on stdin, the defect that hung //#secrets:baseline for eight hours. THE EXIT CODE IS NOT THE VERDICT: expo-doctor exits non-zero whenever any check fails, including for drift alone, so the summary is PARSED from stdout and classified here. FAILS CLOSED with unreadable DOMINATING: a run whose output could not be read cannot honestly report a pass or name a specific peer, and an empty app list is unreadable rather than ok -- the confident zero estate:verify refuses for a worktree list with no records. Pure cores (EXPO_APPS, doctorArgs, parseDoctorSummary, doctorVerdict, describeDoctor) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts against REAL output fixtures from this repo; only the driver spawns npx. Exit 0 ok, 1 a native peer is missing, 3 unreadable. cache:false -- it queries the npm registry and reads live manifests, and a cached verdict is a stale verdict. Run: pnpm exec turbo run expo:doctor.",
