    "//#bump:expo": {
      "description": "Root-level task: align every Expo app's dependencies with the versions the INSTALLED SDK expects -- the reviewable bump //#expo:doctor defers to. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only: it spans both Expo workspaces and belongs to neither. WHY IT EXISTS: //#expo:doctor reports drift and deliberately does NOT block on it, because the fix rewrites dependency versions across the Frozen Stack and a gate born red on 26 patch drifts is one everybody works around -- the adoption failure //#typecheck:scripts and //#knip both document. But deferring to a reviewable bump only means something if the bump is a CAPTURED OP, and it was not: the sole way to do it was `expo install --check` typed by hand, the uncaptured idiom the rule forbids and exactly the shape //#bump:turbo and //#bump:pnpm were created to retire for their own toolchains. THE VERSIONS ARE EXPO'S, NOT OURS, and that is the design difference from //#bump:turbo. bump:turbo takes a target version because turbo is one package and the operator chooses when to move; here the correct version of every Expo package is a FACT the installed SDK already knows, and inventing one would reintroduce the very mismatch expo-doctor exists to find. So this op takes NO version argument: it reads the drift table expo-doctor prints -- name, expected, found -- and applies the EXPECTED column verbatim, range operator included, so an exact pin like react-native 0.83.10 stays exact while expo keeps its tilde. It therefore cannot silently move the SDK beyond what is installed, and re-running //#expo:doctor is the verification. ONLY DECLARED PACKAGES ARE TOUCHED: expo-doctor also reports drift for TRANSITIVE packages no manifest owns, and writing those in would mint a phantom direct dependency -- pinned by a tool, used by nobody, forever. EXACT-OCCURRENCE CONTRACT, inherited from bump:turbo: each package must appear as a declared key exactly once, so zero (the plan disagrees with the file) and two (dependencies and devDependencies both declare it, and a blind replace would rewrite the wrong one) both THROW rather than writing a manifest nobody predicted. DRY-RUN BY DEFAULT, matching //#deps:reconcile, //#worktree:preserve and every repair:* task -- --execute is the only consent, so an accidental invocation cannot rewrite a manifest. IDEMPOTENT by construction, so a second run is a verifiable no-op. Pure cores (planExpoBump, applyManifestVersions) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts against a REAL driver-app manifest fixture, including the catalog: protocol a naive semver rewrite would destroy; only the driver spawns pnpm. Exit 0 ok, 1 a manifest could not be rewritten as planned, 3 nothing to do. cache:false -- it reads live manifests and mutates them, and a cached verdict is a stale verdict. Run: pnpm exec turbo run bump:expo [-- --execute].",
