    "//#worktree:stale-gate": {
      "description": "Fail when a worktree is FINISHED and IDLE -- merged, clean, pushed and untouched past RECENT_IDLE_THRESHOLD_HOURS. Exists because closing t1-wt2-cf-beacon-no-transform reported ahead=117 contained=true idleH=604: every commit already in origin/develop, untouched 25 days, with six siblings identical. The predicate bug that REFUSED them is fixed (#550); nothing made anyone RECLAIM them, so residue accumulated until a census happened to be read. ONE THRESHOLD, NOT TWO: close-worktree.ts owns RECENT_IDLE_THRESHOLD_HOURS=24 as the LIVE/STALE line, worktree:close refuses to delete a live worktree, and this gate insists a stale one be reclaimed -- two halves of one invariant, sharing one constant so they cannot drift into an unresolvable state where a worktree is gate-flagged and close-refused at once. NOT stale ALONE: 38 of 48 worktrees here exceed 24h idle, so failing on all of them would be born red, make every branch unmergeable simultaneously, and teach everyone to ignore it -- the documented reason //#typecheck:scripts stayed ungated until zero. Long-lived idle WIP is legitimate; finished work nobody reclaimed is not. CLOSEABILITY IS DELEGATED to decideClose, never re-derived: a second opinion is how pr-follow/pr-automerge carried one bug twice and how sync:worktrees, worktree:close and `git branch -d` were each wrong about the same question. RUNS LOCALLY BY DESIGN -- a CI runner has zero worktrees, so a CI version would inspect an empty set and pass forever. cache:false (reads live reflogs). Run: pnpm exec turbo run worktree:stale-gate.",
      "cache": false
