    "//#terminal:census": {
      "description": "Root-level task: print the terminal-registry ceiling and the next free number. READ-ONLY. The registry supersedes the sync:worktrees census entirely: that census reads `git worktree list`, the LOCAL worktrees of ONE machine, so each of the two laptops sharing this remote sees only its own half of the sequence (the MacBook reported t77 while WSL2 was at t88, and t78 was cut on top of a terminal in use for ten allocations -- the same class forced t16 -> t19 three months earlier). It also FORGETS: closing a worktree drops the high-water, so a burned number gets re-issued. Terminal refs are never deleted, so the remote ceiling only rises. Reads refs/remotes/ LOCALLY via for-each-ref, so no network round-trip; run sync:worktrees first if the refs are stale. cache:false -- the answer changes whenever any machine claims. Run: pnpm exec turbo run terminal:census.",
      "cache": false
    },
    "//#terminal:claim": {
      "description": "Root-level task: claim the next terminal number and print the conventional worktree directory name. Exists because the pure core (terminal-registry.ts) was complete and unit-tested but NO TASK CALLED IT -- the rule was correct, verified and unreachable, so every claim was hand-typed from memory. Hand-typing lost three guarantees the core specifies: (1) the claim push carried NO --force-with-lease, so a concurrent claim from the other laptop would be silently overwritten, where git documents the lease as an atomic compare-and-swap and an EMPTY expected value as create-if-absent; (2) reads went over the network via ls-remote instead of local for-each-ref; (3) nothing guaranteed the claim object was unique, and two machines pushing an IDENTICAL object both report 'Everything up-to-date' and exit 0, defeating the lease -- so the blob carries host + timestamp. FETCHES BEFORE LEASING (the documented CI shape) because the lease compares against the last fetch. FAILS CLOSED on an empty registry after a successful fetch rather than answering t1, which would re-issue a burned number. cache:false -- it mutates the remote. Run: pnpm exec turbo run terminal:claim -- <slug>.",
