    "//#gate:coverage": {
      "description": "Root-level task: the pre-push COVERAGE gate -- the recursive test:coverage run plus the 90/90/90/90 merge, serialized across worktrees. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only: it spans every workspace and the host lock, so it belongs to no package. It existed as a committed root script from t86 but was NEVER a turbo task, reachable only through a bash string in .pre-commit-config.yaml -- the same hole //#lint:scripts, //#typecheck:scripts and //#lint:e2e each closed, where a check exists in principle and has no rediscoverable invocation. Registering it means the hook, the terminal and any future CI step run the identical op. TWO ROOT CAUSES IT NOW CARRIES. (1) OUTPUT VOLUME, from t86: the hook streamed every workspace vitest run through pre-commit captured pipe, the pipe filled, and a write to a non-blocking fd returned EAGAIN, killing the push three times on 2026-08-05 while the tests were PASSING. Output goes to a log file under HOME and is replayed IN FULL on failure, so the operator sees more than the streaming gate showed, not less. A quiet reporter was tried first and provably cannot work: test:coverage scripts are compound shell strings and recursive pnpm does not forward trailing args into them, so the unit test asserted intent rather than behaviour and passed while the push failed identically. (2) HOST PORTABILITY, from t122 (2026-08-15): the gate spawned flock(1), which is util-linux and does not exist on macOS -- so it had NEVER run on any Mac in the estate. spawnSync failed ENOENT, the child never started, the log stayed 0 bytes, and a status-coalesce collapsed never-ran into exited-non-zero, printing a coverage-FAILED banner with nothing under it. Every push from every Mac was blocked by what looked like a red suite and was a missing binary. The lock is now taken IN-PROCESS with mkdir, which is atomic on every filesystem including network ones, where O_EXCL is documented to race; the lock is released in a finally so a throw cannot strand the estate; and a spawn error is reported as a HOST problem with its own exit code (4) instead of masquerading as a failing test. Staleness uses mtime with a threshold set far beyond any physically possible run, because mkdir stamps mtime once and a heartbeat is impossible while spawnSync blocks the event loop -- a late reap of a dead holder is strictly safer than killing a live one. Contention QUEUES rather than fails: six worktree terminals share one host and concurrent coverage runs starve each other. Pure cores (coverageArgs, mergeArgs, gateLogPath, gateExitCode, decideLockAttempt, isStale, lockDirPath, spawnFailureMessage) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts, with an architectural guard reading the driver source so a future edit cannot silently shell out again. Exit 0 ok, 1 coverage, 3 merge, 4 host. cache:false -- it runs the suite and mutates host lock state; a cached verdict is a stale verdict. Run: pnpm exec turbo run gate:coverage.",
