    "//#secrets:baseline": {
      "description": "Root-level task: refresh (or audit) the tracked detect-secrets baseline that the pre-commit hook reads via --baseline .secrets.baseline. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only: it scans the whole tree, so it belongs to no workspace package. WHY IT EXISTS: the hook blocks only findings absent from the baseline, so a branch that adds files containing credential-SHAPED but synthetic strings (randomBytes test passwords, RFC 2606 .invalid hosts, SHA-256 topology hashes, a task literally named guard:local-secrets) blocks EVERY worktree push until the baseline is refreshed -- observed 2026-07-23 when the security-guard arc landed seven scripts without regenerating it. Previously this refresh was an uncaptured CLI incantation, free to drift from the hook own flags; this task is the single definition. Scans IN PLACE with --baseline (never shell redirection, which silently discards the is_secret labels a prior audit recorded) and mirrors the hook exclude list. NOT a suppression tool: a real credential must be removed and rotated, never baselined -- run -- --audit to label each finding before committing. INTERACTIVE (2026-08-18): --audit is a TUI that prints one finding and blocks on stdin for (y)es/(n)o/(s)kip/(q)uit, and without this key turbo captures the child stdout to prefix it, so the prompt arrived buffered and unanswerable -- a run hung EIGHT HOURS AND FIVE MINUTES before it was killed, with no timeout and no diagnostic. The fix is the key turborepo provides for exactly this, already used by eas:build:ios:interactive for the same reason (its Apple device prompts cannot be answered through a pipe either); a TTY guard in the script was written first and was a workaround for a capability the tool already had. cache:false (mutates a tracked file, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run secrets:baseline [-- --audit].",
