    "//#guard:local-secrets:baseline": {
      "description": "Root-level task: regenerate scripts/local-secret-guard.config.json, the SHA-256 hashes of production hostnames that must never appear in tracked source. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only. The hash is DERIVED AT RUNTIME from the live Railway value via the same resolver the ops tasks use, so the plaintext hostname is never typed into a tracked file -- a guard whose own config leaked the topology it protects would be self-defeating. Mirrors the hashed_secret field in .secrets.baseline. Reports a hash prefix only, never the hostname. cache:false (live network read). Run: pnpm exec turbo run guard:local-secrets:baseline.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#doctor": {
      "description": "Root-level task: READ-ONLY report of what this MACHINE can and cannot do, with an actionable fix per gap. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only: it inspects the host and the clone, so it belongs to no workspace package. ROOT CAUSE THIS CLOSES: every defect found on 2026-08-15/16 surfaced as a DISGUISED RUNTIME FAILURE, discovered mid-task by whoever tripped over it. Absent pre-commit hooks let commit 88357d0 -- a change made entirely of age key material -- through with zero secret scanning, and nothing said so. An absent container runtime printed a coverage-FAILED banner over an empty log. A Linux-only flock binary masqueraded as a red test suite and blocked every push from every Mac for the life of that script. A machine that was not yet an age recipient learned so from a refusal in the middle of unrelated work. And //#git:merge-drivers, documented as run-once-per-clone, has never been surfaced by anything at all -- a declared-but-unregistered merge driver silently does nothing. The 2026 answer to this class is a doctor command: modular checks, a structured pass/fail report, and a remediation per finding, run BEFORE the work instead of discovered during it. CAPABILITIES, NOT FILES. The obvious check -- is there a .env -- would be WRONG and would red-flag a working machine. This laptop built 13 workspaces, passed the 90/90/90/90 gate against Testcontainers and shipped five PRs to production with NO copied .env, because compose.yaml hardcodes DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL and the OIDC trio itself while compose:env generates the only values .env must carry. So the question is never which file is missing but which capability is unavailable and why. THREE STATES, and the third is load-bearing: ready, broken, blocked. BROKEN is fixable on this machine right now and exits non-zero; BLOCKED waits on an action only another host can take, such as the one-time env:encrypt from a machine holding the plaintext, and exits ZERO -- failing on somebody else pending action would keep the doctor red for days and train the operator to ignore it, the same adoption reasoning //#knip documents. READ-ONLY BY DESIGN: it diagnoses and never repairs, even though every remediation it prints is a registered op it could invoke. The repo already draws that line -- //#sync:worktrees reports drift and refuses to install, and //#deps:reconcile exists so that invoking it IS the operator deciding. A doctor that silently started Docker, generated an age identity or rewrote git config would be the same violation wearing a helpful face. Reads the git COMMON dir for hooks, since worktrees share one hooks directory, and counts a hook only when pre-commit wrote it, so a hand-rolled file of the same name cannot read as installed. Recipiency is matched by the age PUBLIC key derived from the local identity, never by hostname: a comment naming a host is prose, and prose doing an assertion job is the defect the roster guard was written to end. Pure cores (CAPABILITIES, capabilityStatus, diagnose, remediationFor, overallExit) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts. cache:false -- it reads live host state, and a cached verdict is a stale verdict. Run on any machine, any time: pnpm exec turbo run doctor.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#bootstrap:machine": {
      "description": "Root-level task: make this MACHINE ready to commit safely -- verify the tools the git hooks shell out to, then install EVERY hook type .pre-commit-config.yaml declares. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it configures the clone itself, belongs to no workspace package). ROOT CAUSE THIS CLOSES: every credential guard this repo owns -- detect-secrets, detect-private-key, check-env-files, block-large-binaries -- runs ONLY from pre-commit, and NOTHING installed pre-commit on a fresh clone. So a new machine could clone, install, build and commit with all four guards silently absent. Observed 2026-08-11: commit 88357d0, a change made entirely of age key material, was authored on a fresh machine with zero secret scanning. It passed because nothing ran, not because it was clean -- the same shape as //#lint:scripts and //#lint:e2e, a check that exists in principle and nowhere in practice. THE SUBTLER HALF: the config declares THREE hook types (pre-commit, pre-push for the build/test gates, commit-msg for commitlint) and bare pre-commit install installs only the first, with no default_install_hook_types key to widen it. Fixing it by hand on that machine left two of three still missing while looking finished, which is why the required set is DERIVED from the config rather than typed twice and the decision NAMES the missing types instead of reinstalling blindly. Reads the git COMMON dir, never the per-worktree git dir: worktrees share one hooks directory, so the per-worktree path would report hooks missing in every worktree forever. Counts a hook as installed only when pre-commit itself wrote it, so a hand-rolled or husky file of the same name cannot read as installed while running none of the configured guards. TOOLS ARE CHECKED BEFORE HOOKS: installing hooks whose binaries are absent produces a clone that fails at commit time with an opaque error, strictly worse than refusing and naming the remedy. ALSO WIRED AS THE package.json prepare SCRIPT, which is how a fresh clone self-heals without anyone remembering this task exists -- and prepare runs on EVERY pnpm install including all eight CI workflows, none of which pass --ignore-scripts, so the prepare path passes --soft and always exits 0 while CI is detected and skipped outright. Invoked directly it exits non-zero on a blocked machine, because there the operator asked and deserves a real answer. Pure cores (parseDeclaredStages, decideBootstrap, describeFinding) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; only the driver spawns pre-commit. cache:false (mutates the clone hook state, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run bootstrap:machine.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#env:decrypt": {
      "description": "Root-level task: materialize .env on this machine by DECRYPTING the tracked .env.sops.yaml with this host age identity. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (the env file is the repo root env; it belongs to no workspace package). ROOT CAUSE THIS CLOSES: nothing in this repo could PRODUCE a .env. The blocking half was complete and layered -- .gitignore bars .env, the pre-commit check-env-files hook bars committing one, detect-secrets scans the index and //#guard:local-secrets scans the working tree -- but prevention without provisioning left exactly one way to start a new machine: copy the plaintext file by hand over AirDrop, USB or chat. That is the 2026-documented anti-pattern (plaintext on N laptops, no rotation path, no record of which host holds which vintage), and it is not a habit anyone chose; it was the only path available. Observed 2026-08-09 on a third machine whose fresh clone legitimately had no .env and could not obtain one from the repo. HOW: SOPS encrypts VALUES only, so field NAMES stay plaintext and the ciphertext diffs and reviews like ordinary config; age supplies the recipient scheme, one keypair per machine, so adding or revoking a laptop is a re-encrypt against .age-recipients rather than a re-copy. FAILS CLOSED, each refusal naming a condition and never a value: missing sops/age binary, missing identity file, missing ciphertext, and -- the destructive one -- an existing .env, which it REFUSES to overwrite because local edits that were never encrypted are unrecoverable. The private identity is passed via SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE in the environment, NEVER as an argv flag, because argv is world-readable in the process table; the decrypted output is written to a mode-600 file and is never echoed to stdout, so plaintext cannot reach scrollback or a CI log. Pure cores (decideBootstrap, describeRefusal, decryptArgs) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; only the driver spawns sops. cache:false (writes secret material outside the task graph; a cached replay would be a stale credential, which is the failure //#prod:db-url exists to prevent). Run on a new machine: pnpm exec turbo run env:decrypt.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false,
      "passThroughEnv": ["SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE"]
    },
    "//#env:encrypt": {
      "description": "Root-level task: re-encrypt the local plaintext .env into the tracked .env.sops.yaml after a credential change, so every other machine picks it up by pulling. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only. This is the WRITE half of the bootstrap pair and the reason rotation stops being a treadmill: one re-encrypt plus one commit replaces hunting through three laptops for stale copies. Writes a SEPARATE output file rather than encrypting in place -- an in-place failure would destroy the only plaintext copy on the machine. FAILS CLOSED when .env is absent rather than emitting an EMPTY ciphertext over a good one, which would silently strip every other machine of its ability to bootstrap and would look like a successful run. Requires no age identity: encryption needs only the PUBLIC recipients in .sops.yaml, so any machine can re-encrypt even before it can decrypt. ALWAYS commit .env.sops.yaml together with .age-recipients and .sops.yaml -- a recipient list that disagrees with the ciphertext locks somebody out silently. cache:false (mutates a tracked file from untracked secret input). Run: pnpm exec turbo run env:encrypt.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#env:recipients": {
      "description": "Root-level task: regenerate .sops.yaml from the tracked .age-recipients list. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only. .age-recipients is the human-edited SSOT (one age PUBLIC key per line, comments allowed); .sops.yaml is GENERATED and must never be hand-edited, because sops reads it to decide who can decrypt and a hand-drift between the two files is invisible until a machine cannot open the ciphertext. Rendering is deterministic, so regenerating with an unchanged list produces an empty diff. Parsing FAILS CLOSED on any malformed entry rather than silently dropping it -- a dropped recipient is a machine that quietly loses access, discovered only later -- and refuses an EMPTY list rather than encrypting to nobody, which sops would otherwise accept as a valid no-recipient rule. The regex admits only age PUBLIC keys, so an age PRIVATE key pasted into the list by mistake is rejected instead of being published to git. After changing recipients ALWAYS run env:encrypt and commit all three files together. REVOCATION IS NOT RETROACTIVE: removing a line stops future decryption but git history still holds the old ciphertext that machine could already read, so a revocation must be followed by rotating the underlying credentials. Pure cores (parseRecipients, renderSopsConfig, validateAgeRecipient) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts. cache:false (mutates a tracked file). Run: pnpm exec turbo run env:recipients.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#knip": {
      "description": "Root-level task: dead-code gate -- report unused files, exports and dependencies across all 13 workspaces (knip). Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only: the analysis is whole-graph and cross-workspace, so a per-package task would see each package in isolation and mark every cross-workspace export dead. ROOT CAUSE THIS CLOSES: nothing in this repo could see a dead export. SUPPORTED_LOCALES in ops-web src/lib/i18n.ts had ZERO consumers repo-wide, and because nothing reported it, its sibling Locale union drifted into three independently hand-written copies (the union, the array, and the parseLocale equality check) where adding a third locale breaks NONE of them at compile time. eslint no-unused-vars cannot see this: it works within a file, never across the import graph. ADOPTION STAGE 1 of 3 (knip.dev/guides/adopt-gradually): every rule in knip.jsonc is warn, so this reports without failing while the config gaps are tuned -- on a legacy codebase the first run mostly surfaces unresolvable entry points and plugin-less deps, not real findings, and gating on that noise would make the branch unmergeable and teach the team to ignore the tool. Burn-down order is files -> dependencies -> exports (one unused FILE hides its own exports and deps, so files must go first), promoting each rule to error as it reaches zero, then ratcheting the remainder with --max-issues the way token-literal-ratchet does. NOT wired into __ci_fast__ yet: that happens in stage 3, when the first rule turns error. cache:false -- a pass/fail report over working-tree state, matching codemod:check and guard:local-secrets. Run: pnpm exec turbo run knip [-- --files | --dependencies | --exports].",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#sync:worktrees": {
      "description": "Root-level task: fast-forward every git worktree local branch to its remote tracking branch, SAFELY. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). Fast-forwards only when a branch is purely behind (behind>0, ahead=0); REFUSES and warns on true divergence (ahead>0 AND behind>0), never force-merging/resetting, so unpushed local commits can never be lost. The autonomous pipeline converges the REMOTE branches but git never auto-updates LOCAL worktree branches, so this closes that gap. cache:false (side-effecting git operation, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run sync:worktrees.",
      "cache": false
