    "//#worktree:preserve": {
      "description": "Root-level task: PRESERVE uncommitted work in every git worktree that cannot yet be closed -- the rescue half worktree:close deliberately refuses to own. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). THE GAP IT CLOSES, measured: worktree:close REFUSES on dirty, which is correct -- it must never delete unpushed work -- but a refusal is not a resolution, and three TOOLCHAIN worktrees have carried staged files since 2026-07-11 with no captured way to rescue them. Those worktrees are ALSO pnpm-blocked (11.13.0 yanked), so they cannot install, cannot run a gate, and cannot reach a normal commit path at all. WHAT IT DOES: for each target, classify the dirty set, commit it to a wip/ branch and push, then VERIFY by asking git how many files actually landed -- an equality gate, so a surplus fails as loudly as a shortfall, because a plan that disagrees with the repository is a defect either way. Push is WITHHELD on shortfall: a partial preservation that reports success is worse than a refusal. --no-verify on commit and push is deliberate and narrow: WIP by definition cannot pass lint, and the alternative is that the work stays unrescued forever. Detached HEAD refuses outright. Per-target ISOLATION: one worktree throwing records a failed outcome and the sweep continues, and failed is distinct from shortfall because the work is uncommitted-but-intact rather than possibly gone. DRY-RUN BY DEFAULT, matching deps:reconcile and repair:*; --execute is the only consent, and a dry run is unreachable-by-construction rather than merely skipped -- the port call log is empty. Graded exits: 0 ok, 1 shortfall, 2 usage, 3 nothing-to-do, 4 failed. Pure cores (worktree-preserve.ts, worktree-preserve-runner.ts with an INJECTED write port, worktree-preserve-cli.ts argv/target assembly) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts at 73 tests; only the driver touches git. cache:false (side-effecting git mutation, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run worktree:preserve [-- <worktree-path>] [-- --execute].",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#worktree:close": {
      "description": "Root-level task: close a git worktree SAFELY -- remove the worktree and delete its local branch, but ONLY when nothing can be lost. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). REFUSES on any loss risk and reports every reason at once: primary-clone, no-upstream, unpushed (ahead>0), dirty (any modified or untracked file), unmerged (commits not contained in origin/develop). Never passes --force, -D or -f, so git own merged-check is the last line of defence. Complements sync:worktrees, which fast-forwards but has no removal path -- closing a worktree was previously a hand-rolled git idiom, not a captured op. Decision core (scripts/close-worktree.ts) and parsers (scripts/worktree-close.ts) are pure and unit-tested; only the driver touches git. cache:false (side-effecting git mutation, no cacheable output). --done (2026-08-11) waives ONLY the recency guard, and ONLY when the work is already contained in origin/develop. Recency refuses a worktree whose per-worktree HEAD reflog shows activity within 24h, because a merged-and-clean worktree can still be the LIVE directory a terminal is coding in -- a real near-miss (t20-wt1) that keeps the guard. But it also made a FINISHED session uncloseable: t106-wt1-driver-delete-audit had every PR merged, production verified, contained=true, dirty=0, and was refused purely on idleH=0 because the operator was still at the keyboard. Waiting out 24h to reclaim finished work is a treadmill, and re-running later is the kind of uncaptured follow-up that gets forgotten. The 2026 industry criteria for safe worktree removal are merged + clean + not-primary; recency appears in none of them. idleHours is a PROXY for work-may-be-in-flight, containment is DIRECT EVIDENCE the work is finished -- a proxy must not outrank direct evidence, yet containment alone cannot waive it either, since the t20 near-miss was ALSO contained. So the operator supplies the one fact neither git nor a clock can know (the session is over) and the machine still demands its own evidence before honouring it. Every other guard still refuses: dirty, unpushed, no-upstream, primary-clone. Mirrors --retired exactly (opt-in, order-independent, unknown flags ignored), and the state line prints done= so a permitted close is auditable. Run: pnpm exec turbo run worktree:close -- <worktree-path> [--retired] [--done].",
