    },
    "//#deps:reconcile": {
      "description": "Root-level task: RECONCILE dependency drift across every git worktree -- the remediation half that //#sync:worktrees deliberately refuses to own. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees, not a per-package task). WHY A SEPARATE TASK: sync:worktrees documents 'Reports, never heals: the operator decides when to install', and its wiring guard asserts the source contains no frozen-lockfile or force install, because an implicit install across 45 worktrees on a 9.7GiB box is destructive. Bolting a --heal flag onto the detector would have turned that green contract red and invited deleting it -- suppressing a guard to fit a feature. Invoking THIS task is the operator deciding. THE GAP IT CLOSES, measured: a 45-worktree census reported 12 deps-stale under SIX distinct pnpm messages with no captured way to act on any of them, and seven of the last ten commits to pnpm-workspace.yaml are security override-floor raises, each of which stales every worktree at once -- drift is the steady state here, not an incident, and detection alone leaves twelve permanently yellow lines an operator learns to scroll past. INSTRUMENT: the frozen-lockfile install, chosen for two properties pnpm documents. It never generates a lockfile, so it cannot rewrite pnpm-lock.yaml and cannot resurrect the auto-install treadmill that verifyDepsBeforeRun:warn exists to suppress; and it FAILS when the lockfile is out of sync, so its outcome CLASSIFIES -- exit 0 means the tree merely lagged an already-correct lockfile, ERR_PNPM_OUTDATED_LOCKFILE means lockfile and manifests genuinely disagree, which is a human commit and never an auto-heal. Attempting the heal is therefore the discriminator, replacing prose-matching over six message strings any pnpm release may reword. Deliberately NOT pnpm ci: it purges first, and re-downloading 1807 packages across 45 worktrees is worse than the drift. DRY-RUN BY DEFAULT, matching repair:* and intake:redrive and the 2026 remediation-CLI convention; --execute is the only consent, parsed by node:util parseArgs so an unknown flag THROWS rather than yielding a confident no-op, and a positional path can never be mistaken for permission. NEVER heals a toolchain-blocked worktree: pnpm refuses to install v11.13.0 at all, so retrying there is a guaranteed-failing loop on every run forever. Sequential by design -- concurrent installs against one content-addressable store are safe (store operations are atomic) but memory contention on a 9.7GiB box is not, per the same SSOT that pins broad gates to concurrency 1. Every child is bounded at 300s with SIGTERM; one worktree failing records its outcome and the sweep continues. Graded exits: 0 ok, 1 divergent (go fix a lockfile and commit it), 2 usage, 3 failed (investigate the tool or network and re-run) -- failed DOMINATES divergent because a sweep that failed partway is untrustworthy. Pure cores (deps-reconcile.ts, deps-reconcile-runner.ts with an INJECTED spawn, deps-reconcile-cli.ts argv/target assembly) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; the probe itself is the shared adapter worktree-deps-probe.ts, which sync:worktrees also wraps. cache:false (side-effecting install, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run deps:reconcile [-- <worktree-path>] [-- --execute].",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#git:merge-drivers": {
      "description": "Root-level task: declare and REGISTER the keep-theirs merge driver for GENERATED files. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it configures the repository itself, belongs to no workspace package). ROOT CAUSE THIS ELIMINATES: .secrets.baseline is generated -- detect-secrets rewrites generated_at on every refresh and shifts line_number whenever any scanned file gains a line -- so two branches that both touched it conflict on content carrying no meaning. It conflicted twice in one session on nothing but a timestamp, c074d43 shows another terminal spending a whole commit on the same churn, and five of the last twelve commits touching that file are develop merge-downs paying the same tax. Across fifty-plus live worktrees that is a standing cost on every merge, and hand-resolving a generated file is a treadmill by definition. git already solves this: .gitattributes declares the path, git config holds the driver body, and keep-theirs writes the incoming side (cp -f %B %A). Taking THEIRS is correct for this file specifically -- origin/develop is the integration truth, and the detect-secrets pre-commit hook plus //#secrets:baseline regenerate the line numbers locally straight after, so a stale timestamp self-heals while a hand-resolved conflict does not. WHY A TASK AND NOT A DOC: .gitattributes is versionable but merge.<name>.driver lives in git config, which is NOT versionable -- a declared-but-unregistered driver silently does nothing, so the registration must be an executable, rediscoverable op rather than a line in a setup guide nobody runs. Writes to --local, never --global: a driver in a developer's global config would silently change merge behaviour in unrelated projects. In this repo git config --local resolves to the SHARED .git/config (extensions.worktreeConfig is unset), so one run covers every worktree. Idempotent -- a second run reports already-registered, and a CHANGED driver body counts as unregistered so it is re-applied rather than left stale. SAFETY: the driver DISCARDS one side, so the allowlist is a short literal asserted list, never a glob; pnpm-lock.yaml is deliberately excluded because a wrong resolution there is a real dependency change, not noise. Pure cores (gitattributesContent, driverConfigEntries, gitConfigArgs, isRegistered) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; only the registrar touches git. cache:false (mutates git config and a tracked file, no cacheable output). Run once per clone: pnpm exec turbo run git:merge-drivers.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#assert:parses": {
      "description": "Root-level task: assert that one or more TypeScript files PARSE, EXPORT the intended names, and (for specs) declare at least N tests -- every question answered from the AST, never by matching text. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it takes arbitrary paths and belongs to no workspace package). ROOT CAUSE THIS ELIMINATES: authoring-time gates verified freshly-written files by counting characters, and it failed three ways in one session. Brace counting balanced EXACTLY on a file containing a double else that does not parse, so the gate printed OK on broken source and the defect surfaced minutes later from lint:scripts as a parsing error far from the write that caused it; the same counting rejects the perfectly valid const brace = '{'. Export checks by substring were satisfied by the phrase appearing in a COMMENT and defeated by export { x }, by export const, and by every exported type. And a test count of /\\n  it\\(/g encoded TWO-SPACE INDENTATION as a contract -- verified scoring ZERO on a file holding four real tests written flush-left, deeply indented, as it.each and as the test alias. None is fixable with a better pattern, because none of the properties is lexical. ts.createSourceFile already builds the tree, so parseDiagnostics answers syntax, an export walk answers surface, and a CallExpression walk answers test count, all immune to formatting. NOT a substitute for //#typecheck:scripts or //#lint:scripts, which run in CI and check far more: this answers is-this-well-formed in milliseconds AT THE MOMENT OF WRITING, so a malformed heredoc fails immediately instead of surfacing from a gate later. Deliberately NOT wired into __ci_fast__ for that reason -- CI already covers these properties more thoroughly, and a duplicate gate there would add runtime without adding signal. Uses the raw compiler API rather than ts-morph: ts-morph wraps the same API this calls directly, so the dependency would buy nothing for a read-only traversal. OUTPUT IS A JSON ENVELOPE on stdout -- {event, file, reasons, agent_action} -- so an orchestrator routes on reason codes instead of parsing prose, the rule gate:agent already follows with NDJSON. FAILS CLOSED: an unreadable file is never a pass. cache:false (a pass/fail check over working-tree state, matching lint:scripts and test:scripts). Run: pnpm exec turbo run assert:parses -- --exports=foo,bar --min-tests=12 scripts/thing.test.ts.",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
    },
    "//#estate:verify": {
      "description": "Root-level task: ASSERT the worktree estate is clean, and say exactly why when it is not. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans every worktree, belonging to no workspace package). WHY IT EXISTS: verifying the estate was three commands read by human eyes -- git worktree list, git stash list, git status --porcelain -- the same hand-rolled-idiom shape worktree:close was created to retire. It also nearly shipped a false report: the worktree:sweep unmerged refusal, not those three commands, is what revealed PR #565 was still OPEN after a session summary had already called it deployed. FOUR PROPERTIES, not the three a hand check sees: dirty tree, unpushed commits, stashes, AND stale/locked worktrees -- git worktree list --porcelain reports prunable (gitdir points at a non-existent location) and locked, and GitLab records that git 2.16 fails badly when stale worktrees exist. STDOUT IS DATA, STDERR IS HUMANS: exactly one NDJSON estate_verified event on stdout so an orchestrator pipes to jq without stripping prose, and the readable summary on stderr -- the same split pr-automerge documents after a transient gh message landed in front of JSON and was misclassified as a permanent contract violation. GRADED EXITS so a caller branches on the failure MODE: 0 clean, 1 not clean, 2 usage, 3 unreadable, 4 stale. Four is the If-Match/412 case, reached only when --expect-digest is supplied and the estate has moved since the caller observed it: nothing is broken and no worktree needs attention, so the remediation is to re-read and re-plan rather than to repair. It exists because estate_digest let a caller RECORD what it observed while nothing let it BIND an action to that observation, and a check performed separately from the act is the split compare-and-swap closes -- two laptops and many worktrees mutate this estate concurrently. FAILS CLOSED at 3 -- an estate that cannot be queried must never report clean with 0 checked, the confident-zero hazard stack:stop and docker:reclaim both guard. The state shape is BRANDED (WorktreeStateSchema.brand), so the schema is the only constructor and a raw object literal cannot reach the classifier -- verified: tsc rejects it with Property '[$brand]' is missing. Pure cores (classifyEstate, describeEstate, estateTelemetry, parseWorktreeRecords, parseEstateArgv) are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; only the driver touches git, always via execFileSync with an argv array and no shell. cache:false -- the answer changes with every edit in every worktree, and a cached verdict is a stale verdict, which is the exact failure it exists to catch. Run: pnpm exec turbo run estate:verify [-- --quiet].",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": false
      ,"passThroughEnv": ["TRACEPARENT"]
    },
    "//#worktree:sweep": {
      "description": "Root-level task: batch-close every merged worktree in the estate in one rediscoverable op. Prefix //# ensures repo-root-only (it spans all worktrees). Composes the pure planSweep candidate selector (scripts/sweep-worktrees.ts) with the EXISTING worktree:close pipeline -- decideClose/closePlan/resolveCloseInput are imported verbatim, never reimplemented, so a swept close is byte-identical to a single worktree:close and inherits every guard: it REFUSES per-candidate on primary-clone, no-upstream, unpushed, dirty, or unmerged (not contained in origin/develop). State is gathered AT ATTEMPT TIME, so a branch a concurrent terminal advanced since any census is re-checked and correctly refused -- the reason the sweep drives the guard rather than pre-trusting a snapshot. --dry-run prints the per-candidate verdict + a removed/refused/kept footer without mutating. Complements worktree:close (one path) and sync:worktrees (fast-forward, no removal). Pure core + report are unit-tested under //#test:scripts; only the driver touches git. Never passes --force/-D/-f. cache:false (side-effecting git mutation, no cacheable output). Run: pnpm exec turbo run worktree:sweep [-- --dry-run].",
      "cache": false
    },
    "tokens:build": {
      "description": "Package task: each consuming app regenerates its OWN design-token output from the @fleet/design-tokens SSOT (ops-web writes src/app/globals.css via node scripts/build-tokens.mts write; the RN apps will emit their theme tokens the same way). NOT a //# root task -- it is implemented per package so every app owns and rewrites a file inside its own boundary (2026 monorepo rule: no cross-package reach). dependsOn @fleet/design-tokens#build so the emitter dist exists before the .mts imports it via its public ./emit-ops-web subpath. cache:false (side-effecting file write). Run: pnpm exec turbo run tokens:build --filter=@fleet/ops-web.",
      "dependsOn": ["@fleet/design-tokens#build"],
      "cache": false
    },
    "tokens:lint": {
      "description": "Package task: RATCHET guard forbidding any INCREASE in raw Tailwind colour literals inside JSX className attributes. Complements tokens:check, which proves globals.css matches the token SSOT but says nothing about whether COMPONENTS consume the roles -- so a page migrated to semantic roles can have a raw text-slate-900 reintroduced days later by another worktree with every gate green (16b0511 did exactly that to a page 5ae52ec had just cleaned). Deliberately NOT a boolean gate: develop carries 274 occurrences across 17 files, and a permanently-red gate gets switched off, which is worse than none. Instead a per-file budget in apps/ops-web/design-token-ratchet.tsv that may only DECREASE. TSV not JSON because every migrating worktree edits it and JSON conflicts on braces and commas when two worktrees migrate different components in parallel, the normal case here. Fails CLOSED: a file absent from the baseline has an implicit budget of zero, so a NEW file carrying literals is a regression, not a silent pass. Pure core is unit-tested in @fleet/design-tokens; only the .mts driver touches the filesystem, the same split as emit-ops-web plus build-tokens.mts. dependsOn @fleet/design-tokens#build so the guard dist exists before the driver imports its public ./literal-guard subpath. cache:false (pass/fail check). Refresh after a migration with -- --tighten, which refuses while a regression is outstanding so it can never launder new debt into the baseline.",
      "dependsOn": ["@fleet/design-tokens#build"],
      "cache": false
    },
    "tokens:check": {
      "description": "Package task: drift guard -- regenerate the app design-token output in-memory and exit 1 when the on-disk file differs from the @fleet/design-tokens SSOT (node scripts/build-tokens.mts check). Implemented per consuming package. dependsOn @fleet/design-tokens#build. cache:false (pass/fail check). Wire into the app CI gate so a stale committed globals.css fails the build. Run: pnpm exec turbo run tokens:check --filter=@fleet/ops-web.",
      "dependsOn": ["@fleet/design-tokens#build"],
      "cache": false
    },
    "dev": {
      "description": "Local dev server (persistent, turbo watch-aware)",
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true,
      "interruptible": true
    },
    "clean": {
      "description": "Remove build artifacts and caches",
      "cache": false
    }
  }
